REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w07","title":"Radio reports, numbers, interviews and group discussions","summary":"The speaking module in practice. How to make a two-minute radio report; how to say numbers so that nobody mishears them, and how to show what they mean; and the qualities and the language that work at an interview, in a group discussion, and when you introduce speakers or invite questions.","n":7,"lectures":[{"code":"7.1","title":"Making a radio report | presentation skills, articulation & confident communication","video":"bxobbm5yhm4","seconds":963},{"code":"7.2","title":"Presenting numbers that reveal the truth about girls education in India","video":"OF4F9TFqmrg","seconds":1585},{"code":"7.3","title":"Presenting numbers 2 : street vendors in chennai during covid, 2019","video":"2nbouKJ0J1o","seconds":1502},{"code":"7.4","title":"At an interview | preparation, confidence & strategies for success","video":"KH1OHK4Y6ao","seconds":1617},{"code":"7.5","title":"Group discussion- I | communication, teamwork & strategies for effective participation","video":"N3CRoxMLIqM","seconds":1575},{"code":"7.6","title":"Group discussion- II | introducing speakers, inviting questions & building group presence","video":"D7kxJ__rqaM","seconds":1318}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"making-a-radio-report","level":2,"title":"Making a radio report"},{"id":"what-makes-a-presentation-good","level":3,"title":"What makes a presentation good"},{"id":"why-radio-is-the-hardest-test","level":3,"title":"Why radio is the hardest test"},{"id":"two-rules-for-every-report","level":3,"title":"Two rules for every report"},{"id":"the-activity-sunita-williams-in-space","level":3,"title":"The activity: Sunita Williams in space"},{"id":"presenting-numbers-1-say-them-clearly","level":2,"title":"Presenting numbers 1: say them clearly"},{"id":"a-number-misheard","level":3,"title":"A number misheard"},{"id":"numbers-and-letters-that-sound-alike","level":3,"title":"Numbers and letters that sound alike"},{"id":"words-that-travel-with-numbers","level":3,"title":"Words that travel with numbers"},{"id":"reading-digits-a-matter-of-culture","level":3,"title":"Reading digits: a matter of culture"},{"id":"listeners-are-waiting-for-numbers","level":3,"title":"Listeners are waiting for numbers"},{"id":"time-do-not-overpack","level":3,"title":"Time: do not overpack"},{"id":"radio-report-the-education-of-girls-in-india","level":2,"title":"Radio report: the education of girls in India"},{"id":"the-data","level":3,"title":"The data"},{"id":"the-model-report","level":3,"title":"The model report"},{"id":"one-more-data-set","level":3,"title":"One more data set"},{"id":"presenting-numbers-2-stress-and-relative-values","level":2,"title":"Presenting numbers 2: stress and relative values"},{"id":"teen-or-ty-the-stress-decides","level":3,"title":"-teen or -ty: the stress decides"},{"id":"numbers-in-relation-to-one-another","level":3,"title":"Numbers in relation to one another"},{"id":"radio-report-chennai-s-street-vendors","level":2,"title":"Radio report: Chennai’s street vendors"},{"id":"the-data-and-how-to-say-it","level":3,"title":"The data, and how to say it"},{"id":"the-model-answer","level":3,"title":"The model answer"},{"id":"walking-an-extra-mile-a-wedding-in-nigeria","level":3,"title":"Walking an extra mile: a wedding in Nigeria"},{"id":"at-an-interview","level":2,"title":"At an interview"},{"id":"why-interviews-happen","level":3,"title":"Why interviews happen"},{"id":"two-film-interviews","level":3,"title":"Two film interviews"},{"id":"five-qualities-of-a-successful-candidate","level":3,"title":"Five qualities of a successful candidate"},{"id":"group-discussion-1-behaviour-and-language","level":2,"title":"Group discussion 1: behaviour and language"},{"id":"what-goes-wrong-in-groups","level":3,"title":"What goes wrong in groups"},{"id":"advantages-of-a-gd","level":3,"title":"Advantages of a GD"},{"id":"the-language-of-arrogance","level":3,"title":"The language of arrogance"},{"id":"language-don-ts","level":3,"title":"Language don’ts"},{"id":"good-group-good-language","level":3,"title":"Good group, good language"},{"id":"likely-places-of-error","level":3,"title":"Likely places of error"},{"id":"respect-and-disrespect","level":3,"title":"Respect and disrespect"},{"id":"phrases-for-a-discussion","level":3,"title":"Phrases for a discussion"},{"id":"etiquette","level":3,"title":"Etiquette"},{"id":"group-discussion-2-introducing-and-inviting","level":2,"title":"Group discussion 2: introducing and inviting"},{"id":"introducing-your-own-group","level":3,"title":"Introducing your own group"},{"id":"introducing-a-guest","level":3,"title":"Introducing a guest"},{"id":"welcoming-the-audience","level":3,"title":"Welcoming the audience"},{"id":"inviting-and-answering-questions","level":3,"title":"Inviting and answering questions"},{"id":"what-great-introductions-share","level":3,"title":"What great introductions share"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A radio report</strong> (7.1) has only words. The reporter turns everything seen and heard into words: what happened, what was said, where, and how the place and the people looked. Two rules for any report: say all that is possible <strong>in the given time</strong>, and be <strong>fair to every part</strong> of the topic.</li>\n<li><strong>Numbers are easily misheard</strong> (7.2): <em>13</em> and <em>30</em>, <em>14</em> and <em>40</em>, a <em>five</em> without its last sound. A misheard number can cost lives, as in the air crash the lecture describes. Deliver numbers and the words around them <strong>slowly and carefully</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Words around numbers</strong> (7.2) prepare the listener: <em>much, many, few, mostly, hardly ever, dozens, lakhs, first, last, greater than, per cent, proportion</em>. And when you have 120 seconds, plan for <strong>110</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stress tells <em>-teen</em> from <em>-ty</em></strong> (7.3): <strong>THIR</strong>-ty has the stress on the first syllable; thir-<strong>TEEN</strong> on the second, with a long <em>ee</em>. In <em>UK</em> and <em>USA</em> the stress is on the last letter. Read 7,965 as “seven thousand nine hundred <strong>and</strong> sixty-five”.</li>\n<li><strong>Relative values</strong> (7.2, 7.3): a good report shows how the numbers stand to one another, not only how big they are: <em>whereas, out of, as high as, nearly, a little over, almost half, over a quarter</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>An interview</strong> (7.4) is a formal conversation. Successful candidates are <strong>confident, polite, good listeners, honest and frank without being rude</strong>, and <strong>appropriately dressed</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>A group discussion (GD)</strong> (7.5) builds a team and reaches consensus. Avoid the language of arrogance (<em>I, my opinion, you should, shut up</em>); use the language of team spirit (<em>shall we, can we, may I, you first, we, our team</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Introducing speakers and inviting questions</strong> (7.6): be humble, find the unique fact (not the CV), be complimentary but not exaggerated, and use simple words, short sentences and a slow delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"making-a-radio-report\">Making a radio report</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The best way to learn speaking in any language is to speak in that language.” So to learn presentations, make presentations <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>. Why does it matter so much? Your <strong>first</strong> job comes from your college marks. Your <strong>later</strong> rise comes from how you manage groups, fit into a team and inspire teamwork, and good presentations are part of that <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. Many companies now ask candidates to make a presentation or join a group discussion, in place of a written test or in addition to it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-makes-a-presentation-good\">What makes a presentation good</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The first rule of a presentation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>More than your pronunciation, your clothes or your slides, it is your <strong>knowledge of the subject</strong> and your <strong>preparation</strong> that make a good presentation: the time you spend deciding what you will say first and what you will say next. Pronunciation, grammar and vocabulary are important, “but they are nothing unless you have something to say and unless you know your topic well enough”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-radio-is-the-hardest-test\">Why radio is the hardest test</h3>\n<p>Every reporter uses words, but not equally <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reporter</th>\n<th>How much they depend on words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Television</td>\n<td>least: they have “other props” (the pictures show much of the story)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Newspaper</td>\n<td>more: the reporter writes everything</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Radio</td>\n<td>most: “their dependence upon words is ultimate, is extreme”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A radio reporter must tell us not only what has happened and what has been said, but also <strong>where</strong> it happened, how the place looks, how the people look and what kind of crowd is there. They must “translate everything visual or verbal into words” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>. So a radio reporter must be very <strong>articulate</strong>: able to say clearly, in words, what they mean.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Radio report</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>radio report</strong> is a spoken report in which the listener only hears you: no pictures and no text. Everything the listener needs (events, words, places, people, sights) must come through your words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">What radio has to add (my example)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>On television, the camera shows a crowded railway platform, and the reporter says only: “Huge crowds here today.” On radio, the words must carry the picture:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Where: “I am standing on platform 7 of the central station.”</li>\n<li>What it looks like: “Every bench is taken, and people are sitting on their suitcases.”</li>\n<li>Who: “Most of them are families going home for the festival.”</li>\n<li>What is happening: “Each time a train comes in, the crowd moves towards the doors.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the radio version names the place, the sight, the people and the action. That is “everything visual … into words”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-rules-for-every-report\">Two rules for every report</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Time and fairness</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Say everything that is possible within the given time.</strong> “If you have only 2 minutes, you can say enough only for 2 minutes.”</li>\n<li><strong>Do justice to the topic in that time.</strong> Give “a fair representation to all parts of the topic you are speaking about”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“If you are a good radio reporter, you can practically do anything”: you can talk about anything equally well or better <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-activity-sunita-williams-in-space\">The activity: Sunita Williams in space</h3>\n<p>The practice task uses a NASA video in which <strong>Sunita Williams</strong>, an astronaut of Indian origin, gives a tour of how astronauts live on the space station (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=doN4t5NKW-k\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the video</a>; the slide asks for 0:00–4:00 only) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=820s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:40</a>. People on earth are curious about life without gravity: does tea fly out of the cup? How do you eat, write, stand, sleep, walk or wash? Where does spilled water go? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n<p>While you watch, note (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=415s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:55</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the <strong>names</strong> of people, places and objects;</li>\n<li><strong>words specific to space travel</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>sequence</strong> of sights and actions: what you see first, what next <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>;</li>\n<li>any <strong>differences</strong> between these in space and on the earth, and anything else (for example, do astronauts always wear space gear, or do they sometimes dress as we do at home?).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>How to work, step by step:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Watch first <strong>without subtitles</strong>, even if you understand little. It tunes your ear to the accent and makes you curious <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>.</li>\n<li>Rewind and watch <strong>with subtitles</strong>, to understand the ideas.</li>\n<li>Make a <strong>two-minute</strong> radio report on what you have seen and heard. Do not expect perfection in the first go: begin with 3 minutes, then cut to 2½, then to 2. Two or three attempts are normal <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>.</li>\n<li>You may take notes and speak from them. Radio reporters have this advantage: nobody knows whether they speak from notes or from memory <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Record</strong> your report and share it with friends and reviewers. Revise it in the light of their feedback and do it again <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not lean on a script</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In the beginning it may not be a bad idea to write and then to speak” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=725s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:05</a>). But reduce your dependence on the written text, because “it takes away your naturalness”. Remember the facts, trust yourself, and speak. You may make a mistake or two at first; with practice you learn to use the right facts at the right moment. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide also says that a summary of the video “has been given at the end of this unit”; it is not shown in this lecture.</p>\n<p>The last slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=835s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:55</a>: “Find similar videos and make similar reports and share them with your family, friends and us.” At the practice stage choose <strong>short</strong> videos, not 30- or 40-minute ones <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>. The rewards are many: more confidence, better language, better general knowledge <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"presenting-numbers-1-say-them-clearly\">Presenting numbers 1: say them clearly</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Managers, engineers, scientists, researchers and even entertainers present numbers: years, distances, units, growth, decline. A number said carelessly is misunderstood, and the presentation fails <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. Does it matter whether a listener hears <em>13</em> or <em>30</em>? If you tell a guest, “You can walk from the station to my home in 30 minutes,” perhaps not. But in many fields such a mistake “can lead to serious irreversible losses” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-number-misheard\">A number misheard</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=95s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:35</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Presenting numbers is a problem in spoken communication.</li>\n<li>A Russian pilot once misunderstood a similar instruction before landing in New Delhi and he hit an aircraft taking off with over 200 passengers from there, they both crashed and no one survived.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In the lecturer’s telling, a cargo plane was preparing to land at New Delhi. The air traffic controller told the pilot to descend and wait at 30,000 feet. The pilot, who did not speak English well, heard <strong>13,000</strong>, came down to 13,000 feet, and his wing hit the tail of a plane that had just taken off. No one on either plane survived <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>. The history of air accidents is “full of similar accidents”: easily avoidable, but irreversible, because the pilot or the controller heard one number when another was meant <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The real accident</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer tells the story from memory. The real crash is the Charkhi Dadri mid-air collision, near Delhi, on 12 November 1996. A Saudi Arabian Airlines Boeing 747 that had just left Delhi collided with a Kazakhstan Airlines Ilyushin Il-76 coming in to land, and all 349 people on the two planes died. The Kazakh plane had been told to stay at 15,000 feet but went lower. The airlines and the heights differ from the lecture’s version; the lesson does not: one height said, heard or kept wrongly can cost hundreds of lives.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"numbers-and-letters-that-sound-alike\">Numbers and letters that sound alike</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=335s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:35</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Frequently, people misunderstand 13 for 30, 14 for 40, 15 for 50, etc. or the other way round.</li>\n<li>Many people do not pronounce the last sound of “five” as in “twenty five”, thus causing confusion.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You say</th>\n<th>The listener may hear</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>13, thirteen</td>\n<td>30, thirty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14, fourteen</td>\n<td>40, forty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15, fifteen</td>\n<td>50, fifty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17, seventeen</td>\n<td>70, seventy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19, nineteen</td>\n<td>90, ninety</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(The pairs 90/19 and 17 are from the lecture’s talk at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>.)</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The missing last sound of <em>five</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many speakers in Asian and African countries, in a hurry or by habit, say <em>five</em> as “fy” and <em>twenty-five</em> as “twenty fy” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>. <em>Five</em> has three sounds: <strong>f</strong>, <strong>ai</strong> (as in <em>eye</em>) and <strong>v</strong>. Let the final <strong>v</strong> be heard.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Letters cause the same trouble. Which taxiway, which runway, which sector, which road? Sectors of a city look alike except for the signboard. If you hear <em>P</em> as <em>G</em>, <em>V</em>, <em>C</em>, <em>D</em> or <em>E</em>, “you can go on and on … and you would never reach where you wanted to reach” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>. Such confusions cost “business, friends, and precious time” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>.</p>\n<p>Other faults: sounds missed at the beginning, in the middle or at the end of a word, and words stressed wrongly <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"words-that-travel-with-numbers\">Words that travel with numbers</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some presentations have two or three <strong>axes</strong> (dimensions): time, place, people, rate of growth, several countries at once. The lecture’s example: how COVID-19 affected different countries in different months, with one country highest at first and others overtaking it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>. Then you need words for quantities. They “prepare your listeners to take the number that will follow” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:40</a>: “Then there are words like the following related to the use of numbers”:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Words on the slide</th>\n<th>What they do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>much, many, little, few</td>\n<td>how much or how many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b</td>\n<td>all, none, any, many, mostly, rarely, seldom, often, frequently, hardly ever</td>\n<td>how many of a group, or how often</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c</td>\n<td>billions, dozens, hundreds, litres, meters, numbers, millions, lakhs, pairs, quintals, scores, tons</td>\n<td>groups of numbers and units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d</td>\n<td>last, first, least, most, smaller than, greater than, hardly any</td>\n<td>order and comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e</td>\n<td>percent, portion, proportion, ratio, part, partly, wholly, substantially, several, seldom</td>\n<td>parts of a whole</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In the talk the lecturer adds more order words: <em>second, next, initial, final, ultimate, penultimate, second-best, topmost</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quantifier</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>quantifier</strong> is a word that tells <strong>how much</strong> or <strong>how many</strong>: <em>much, many, little, few, all, none, most</em>. Some other words in the table: a <strong>dozen</strong> is 12; a <strong>score</strong> is 20; a <strong>lakh</strong> is 1,00,000 (one hundred thousand); a <strong>quintal</strong> is 100 kg; <em>penultimate</em> means second from the last.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Not many people came” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Not many people came out of about 200 that had been invited for this talk. Only 67 people turned up, which makes it fewer than maybe one-third.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Not many</em> prepares the listener: a small number is coming.</li>\n<li><em>out of about 200</em> gives the whole.</li>\n<li><em>Only 67</em> gives the part, and <em>only</em> says it is small.</li>\n<li>Check the fraction: 67 out of 200 = 67 ÷ 200 = 0.335 = 33.5%.</li>\n<li>One third is 100 ÷ 3 = 33.3%. So 67 is <strong>just over</strong> a third.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>not many</em>, <em>out of</em> and <em>only</em> set the number in context. The most exact phrase for 67 of 200 is “about a third” (just over a third).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"reading-digits-a-matter-of-culture\">Reading digits: a matter of culture</h3>\n<p>Different places read the same digits differently <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>Common in India</th>\n<th>Common elsewhere</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>one double zero</td>\n<td>one zero zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9444</td>\n<td>nine triple four</td>\n<td>nine four four four</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In a business or formal presentation, be sensitive to such cultural habits <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>. The lecturer’s rule: “you can get away with mistakes of pronunciation and grammar, but you cannot get away with mistakes of culture, with mistakes of discourse” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Per cent</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture says that many Asian speakers pronounce <em>per cent</em> wrongly; it is a two-syllable word with a fixed pronunciation <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>. (A transcript cannot show which syllable the lecturer stresses.) In standard English the stress is on the <strong>second</strong> syllable: per-<strong>CENT</strong>. <em>Per cent</em> means “per 100”: <em>x</em> out of 100.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"listeners-are-waiting-for-numbers\">Listeners are waiting for numbers</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Deliver numbers carefully (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=775s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:55</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Deliver all numbers and related words slowly and carefully.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why this works: people come to a talk <strong>expecting</strong> numbers, so they get them right if you say them right <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>. At a station, “Train number nine six six seven bound for New Delhi is arriving on platform number seven” is understood even in great noise: the mind shuts out the noise and listens for just two slots, the train number and the platform number <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Who is to blame for a mishearing?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Not the listener. Saying that people mishear you on purpose is “a perverted opinion”. “People mishear you because they do not hear you properly. Maybe you have not articulated them properly.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a> Saying numbers well is a skill: “It does not require great intelligence or insight”, only practice and focus <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"time-do-not-overpack\">Time: do not overpack</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A suitcase is made for, say, ten shirts. Some people force in 13, 14 or 15, and even sit on the lid to shut it. It works once or twice; then it breaks at the hinge, at a critical time, when you least expect it. A speech packed with more than its time can hold breaks in the same way <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The Nobel Prize in 40 seconds (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You have 40 seconds to accept a Nobel Prize, and you must say something relevant and interesting.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Do not tell your biography (“even in my mother’s lap I dreamt of this prize”; “I got so many marks in school essays”). All that is redundant (not needed) in 40 seconds.</li>\n<li>Say in one or two sentences what helped you reach this point: something relevant, humble and brief.</li>\n<li>The lecturer imagines what Charles Dickens might have said: “I did not know writing about the poor could make me rich.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one witty, humble, relevant sentence beats a long life story.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keep ten seconds in hand</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=775s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:55</a>: “If you have 120 seconds, then plan for no more than 110 seconds.” Keep those ten free seconds “in your hand” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"radio-report-the-education-of-girls-in-india\">Radio report: the education of girls in India</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Television is multimedia: you see and hear, read and listen. On radio, you <strong>only listen</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>. The great radio broadcasters, with listeners in hundreds of millions, made numbers simple: they used other words to prepare the audience, and then gave the numbers, so there was no misunderstanding <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>. The lecturer opens the topic with the old Indian idea that the entire world is one family: sorrow anywhere reaches all of us sooner or later <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-data\">The data</h3>\n<p>The activity is “State of education of girls in India”, from UNICEF (<a href=\"https://www.unicef.org/rosa/documents/new-era-girls\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the report page</a>) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1165s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:25</a>. The data slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1225s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:25</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a> (source: <a href=\"https://www.unicef.org/rosa/media/10566/file/India.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNICEF India</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>The slide’s figure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Out-of-school children</td>\n<td>6.1 million children aged 6–13 remain out of school; 20 million children do not attend pre-school</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning</td>\n<td>millions of children complete primary schooling without foundational numeracy and literacy skills</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Death soon after birth</td>\n<td>58%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe drinking water</td>\n<td>less than 50% of the population has access to clean drinking water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WASH in schools</td>\n<td>46% of primary schools still don’t have toilets, hand-washing stations and drinking water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open defecation</td>\n<td>26% of the rural population defecates in the open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adolescent girls</td>\n<td>poor nutrition (40% are anaemic), early marriage (27%), early childbearing (8%)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>WASH</strong> means water, sanitation and hygiene. The slide adds that adolescent girls in India face “multiple layers of vulnerability, based on sex, age, caste, socio-economic status and geography”.</p>\n<p>The task (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1325s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:05</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Prepare a two-minute radio report on the state of education and well-being of girls in India.</li>\n<li>For instance, highlight the fact that even those that have gone through the primary schools have hardly learnt anything; or, that many primary schools do not have basic facilities desired by human beings, things like washrooms and drinking water, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Absolute and relative values</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>absolute value</strong> is a number on its own: “46% of primary schools”. A <strong>relative value</strong> shows how numbers stand to one another: this year against another, this country against another, girls against boys. Reading the slide out mechanically “would not fetch you much”, because it says little about relative values <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>. Comparing many points at once (the lecture calls it a <strong>multi-nodal comparison</strong>) works best “when you go beyond what you see”, to the relationships between the numbers <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s example of such a relationship: the dropout rate is higher among girls than among boys, and even those who complete primary school do not get the skills that four, five or six years of schooling ought to give <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-model-report\">The model report</h3>\n<p>The slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:05</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Education is yet to become a universally available facility for girls in India, says a recent report. Nearly three crore girls under the age of thirteen never see the inside of a school even today. But even among those that do, millions come out with a mere certificate, they can neither read nor write even basic things in any language, nor can they do a basic sum in Mathematics.</p>\n<p>The fate of girls is yet to change even in the sector of health. According to this report, over fifty per cent of all children die before completing the age of five. That is also because over half the total population is yet to have access to safe drinking water. Nearly half of all primary schools do not have basic facilities like drinking water, a wash room or a wash basin with water and soap.</p>\n<p>Girls that live a little longer and reach the age of puberty do not seem to be doing any better either. Even their homes do not seem to be a safe place for them. They are subject to a variety of deprivations, discriminations, exploitation and denials. They hardly get enough nutritional food, or a fair share of food and time, as a result of which over one third of them are said to be anemic. Over a quarter of them are forced into marriage before reaching the age of adulthood, and eight of a hundred of these girls become mothers in their early teens. Their world, it seems, is yet to accept them as equals.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Check your report against it “by and large”. It is not the only answer: “You can sure do better than this.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How the model turns percentages into words (my analysis of the lecture’s model)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Slide</th>\n<th>Model report</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>46% of primary schools lack facilities</td>\n<td>“Nearly half”</td>\n<td>46 is a little under 50: fits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>40% of adolescent girls are anaemic</td>\n<td>“over one third”</td>\n<td>40 is more than 33.3: fits (exactly, two in five)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27% marry early</td>\n<td>“Over a quarter”</td>\n<td>27 is more than 25: fits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8% have children early</td>\n<td>“eight of a hundred”</td>\n<td>exact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>under 50% have safe water</td>\n<td>“over half … yet to have access”</td>\n<td>turned round: if under half have it, over half lack it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>death soon after birth 58%</td>\n<td>“over fifty per cent”</td>\n<td>58 is more than 50: fits</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The method, in three steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the nearest simple fraction: a quarter (25%), a third (33.3%), a half (50%).</li>\n<li>Decide whether your number is a little <strong>below</strong> it or <strong>above</strong> it.</li>\n<li>Below: <em>nearly, almost, a little under</em>. Above: <em>over, more than, a little over</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 46% → nearly half; 40% → over a third; 27% → over a quarter.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keep the data’s group and unit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s out-of-school figures are for <strong>children</strong> (6.1 million aged 6–13 out of school; 20 million not in pre-school), not for girls alone, so the model’s “nearly three crore girls” is a loose summary. One crore is 10 million, so 6.1 million is 61 lakh and 20 million is 2 crore. In your own report, keep the group (children or girls) and the unit (million or crore) that the data give.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Indian and international number words (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Indian</th>\n<th>International</th>\n<th>In digits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 lakh</td>\n<td>100 thousand</td>\n<td>100,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 lakh</td>\n<td>1 million</td>\n<td>1,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 crore</td>\n<td>10 million</td>\n<td>10,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100 crore</td>\n<td>1 billion</td>\n<td>1,000,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Use one system in one talk. Listeners abroad may not know <em>lakh</em> and <em>crore</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"one-more-data-set\">One more data set</h3>\n<p>A second activity (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:10</a>): “Look at the following data and present a report for no longer than two-minutes.” The data are in a file linked from the slide; they are not shown in the video. Afterwards, check three things <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did you present <strong>all</strong> that you saw?</li>\n<li>Did you present the <strong>relationships</strong> among them?</li>\n<li>Did you give a relevant <strong>conclusion</strong> or highlight?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Practice with about half a dozen sets of data “will make you a wonderful business presenter”.</p>\n<h2 id=\"presenting-numbers-2-stress-and-relative-values\">Presenting numbers 2: stress and relative values</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Whenever you present statistical data, remember two or three things. The first is <strong>how numbers are pronounced</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. Each number is also a word or a group of words. <em>Sixty-seven</em> is two words and four syllables, six-ty-se-ven, so questions arise of stress (which syllable is prominent), pronunciation and spelling <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. (Syllables and stress were explained in <a href=\"#/english2/w01/rhythm\">Week 1</a>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"teen-or-ty-the-stress-decides\"><em>-teen</em> or <em>-ty</em>: the stress decides</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">-teen and -ty</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In writing, 13 and 30 look very different. In speech, <em>thirteen</em> and <em>thirty</em> both have two syllables, and they differ only in the last one <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>thirty, forty, fifty, sixty</em>: the stress is on the <strong>first</strong> syllable: <strong>THIR</strong>-ty, <strong>FOR</strong>-ty, <strong>FIF</strong>-ty, <strong>SIX</strong>-ty <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</li>\n<li><em>thirteen</em>: the main stress is on the <strong>second</strong> syllable, which has a long vowel, <em>een</em>. “Your teeth should show, your lips should spread”: thir-<strong>TEEN</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The same rule for the rest (the lecture shows 13/30 and lists thirty to sixty; the other rows follow the same rule):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stress on the second syllable</th>\n<th>Stress on the first syllable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>thir-<strong>TEEN</strong> (13)</td>\n<td><strong>THIR</strong>-ty (30)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>four-<strong>TEEN</strong> (14)</td>\n<td><strong>FOR</strong>-ty (40)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>fif-<strong>TEEN</strong> (15)</td>\n<td><strong>FIF</strong>-ty (50)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>six-<strong>TEEN</strong> (16)</td>\n<td><strong>SIX</strong>-ty (60)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>seven-<strong>TEEN</strong> (17)</td>\n<td><strong>SEV</strong>-en-ty (70)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>eigh-<strong>TEEN</strong> (18)</td>\n<td><strong>EIGH</strong>-ty (80)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nine-<strong>TEEN</strong> (19)</td>\n<td><strong>NINE</strong>-ty (90)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress in abbreviations</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When an abbreviation is read letter by letter, the stress is on the <strong>final letter</strong>: U-<strong>K</strong> (the United Kingdom), U-S-<strong>A</strong> (the United States of America). Many of us stress them wrongly <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Clear speech is like correct spelling</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Nobody would write 13 when they mean 30. Saying <em>thirty</em> so that it sounds like <em>thirteen</em> is the same mistake in speech <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>. People are clever and often understand one another in spite of such distortions, “but sometimes it can lead to very serious irreversible accidents”, like the collision in Lecture 7.2 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">A small list, a big return</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>There is not much to learn: 1 to 20, then 21, 22 … up to 100, and then about half a dozen words: <em>thousand, million, billion, dozen, score</em>. “With very little investment, you have very high returns.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"numbers-in-relation-to-one-another\">Numbers in relation to one another</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Schools in three villages (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Only absolute numbers: a plain list, one village after another, each with its count. “We do not say that.”</p>\n<p>In perspective: “In my village there are eight schools, <strong>but</strong> in the one next to mine there are 11 schools. <strong>But unfortunately</strong>, in yet another village as we go on, there are <strong>only</strong> three schools.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Total: 8 + 11 + 3 = 22 schools in the region <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</li>\n<li>They are not evenly spread: 11 in one village, only 3 in another.</li>\n<li>So some sections of the people have easier access to schools than others. That is a real problem in primary education in many developing countries, including India <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the relative words (<em>but, unfortunately, only</em>) show the uneven spread that the total of 22 hides.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The picture also changes with the <strong>parameters</strong> (the things that vary): place, time and other factors, for example “Bihar, primary education, 1993” against “Tamil Nadu, secondary education, 1995” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>. Words made for this job show the change.</p>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Presenting numbers has many aspects.</li>\n<li>One can look at their relative values and get an over all picture of what has happened, and present numbers accordingly.</li>\n<li>One can also present their relative values, using words and expressions like “whereas...”, “on one hand...”, “on the other...”, “out of...”, &quot;as great/high/big, or “as small/low ... as”, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Sentences with these words (my sentences, with the data of the next section):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Whereas</strong>: 3,139 vendors left Royapuram/George Town, <strong>whereas</strong> 1,300 left Kodambakkam.</li>\n<li><strong>On one hand … on the other</strong>: <strong>On one hand</strong>, 11,648 vendors responded; <strong>on the other</strong>, 4,796 could not be reached.</li>\n<li><strong>Out of</strong>: <strong>Out of</strong> 15,265 messages sent, 5,629 were not delivered.</li>\n<li><strong>As many as / as few as</strong> (for counts; for amounts, <em>as high as / as low as</em>): <strong>As many as</strong> 7,965 vendors left the business; <strong>as few as</strong> 21 sent their details by email.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"radio-report-chennai-s-street-vendors\">Radio report: Chennai’s street vendors</h2>\n<p>The activity (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=635s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:35</a>): “See the following report of 21 June 2020 from the City Corporation of Chennai. Now present a two minute radio report. In your report you can highlight numbers, various categories, problems of surveying responses in an unorganized sector, etc.” The <strong>unorganised sector</strong> is work without a regular employer, contract or records, such as street vending.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-data-and-how-to-say-it\">The data, and how to say it</h3>\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:50</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>Say it as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Vendors who left the business</td>\n<td>7,965</td>\n<td>seven thousand nine hundred and sixty-five</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vendors unreachable</td>\n<td>4,796</td>\n<td>four thousand seven hundred and ninety-six</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Street vendors tracked</td>\n<td>23,154</td>\n<td>twenty-three thousand one hundred and fifty-four</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vending zones</td>\n<td>900</td>\n<td>nine hundred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vendors who left the city</td>\n<td>2,509</td>\n<td>two thousand five hundred and nine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vendors who died</td>\n<td>68</td>\n<td>sixty-eight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Messages sent</td>\n<td>15,265</td>\n<td>fifteen thousand two hundred and sixty-five</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undelivered messages</td>\n<td>5,629</td>\n<td>five thousand six hundred and twenty-nine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vendors who left Royapuram/George Town zone, the largest</td>\n<td>3,139</td>\n<td>three thousand one hundred and thirty-nine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vendors who left Kodambakkam zone</td>\n<td>1,300</td>\n<td>one thousand three hundred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Responded / gave bank account details</td>\n<td>11,648</td>\n<td>eleven thousand six hundred and forty-eight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sent account details by email</td>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>twenty-one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sent account details by WhatsApp</td>\n<td>3,468</td>\n<td>three thousand four hundred and sixty-eight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gave account details on the Corporation’s website</td>\n<td>58</td>\n<td>fifty-eight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Had no bank account for receiving cash assistance</td>\n<td>592</td>\n<td>five hundred and ninety-two</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reading a long number aloud</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Say each group with its word (<em>thousand, hundred</em>), and put <strong>and</strong> before the last part: 7,965 = “seven thousand nine hundred <strong>and</strong> sixty-five” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>. The <em>and</em> “signals and tells the listener that now you are coming to the last number”. A single small number needs no <em>and</em>: 68 is just “sixty-eight”. Of its three syllables, <em>six</em> is the prominent one; in <em>sixteen</em> the stress would go to the second syllable <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The key words of this report also need the right stress <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Stressed syllable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NUM</strong>-ber, <strong>VEN</strong>-dors, <strong>BUS</strong>-i-ness, <strong>MES</strong>-sa-ges</td>\n<td>first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>un-<strong>READ</strong>-a-ble, un-<strong>REACH</strong>-a-ble</td>\n<td>second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>un-de-<strong>LIV</strong>-ered</td>\n<td>third</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not claim more than the data say</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>5,629 messages remained undelivered. The phones may have been switched off, or had low power, or low connectivity. But “we cannot say did not have connectivity”: the data do not say whether the phones were connected, or whether the numbers were valid <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>. What you can say, with a relative word: <strong>out of</strong> so many messages sent, a good part remained undelivered <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Vending</strong> means selling, often by <strong>hawking</strong>: going round and calling out, like the ice-cream seller (“Ice cream, ice cream!”) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>. Chennai is divided into 900 vending zones. Each vendor has a licence to sell in their own zone, almost a monopoly there <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>. Vendors disappeared from all zones, but more from some: George Town, Kodambakkam and Royapuram lost many more than the others <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>.</p>\n<p>How to practise <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>: you need not write the report first. Record yourself on your phone or computer, and speak for <strong>no longer than 2 minutes</strong>. In those 2 minutes give the significant absolute numbers <strong>and</strong> the overall picture. Then listen, compare with the model answer, and do it again if you are not happy <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>. Presenting is “both an art and a science, it is a craft”: it comes only through practice <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-model-answer\">The model answer</h3>\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1085s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:05</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:40</a> (the bold words are bold on the slides):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Workers and retail traders in the unorganised sector seem to have been worst affected by Covid-19. In a report released on 21 June, over three months after complete lockdown in India, the situation of street vendors was pretty grim in Chennai. <strong>Nearly</strong> twenty five thousand were contacted so that they could be given some cash assistance.</li>\n<li>But <strong>a little over</strong> five thousand in <strong>different</strong> vending zones could not be reached. No one knew how they were managing.</li>\n<li>Only <strong>a little under</strong> twelve thousand, that is <strong>almost half</strong> of all vendors tracked down, responded to a short message seeking the details of their bank accounts.</li>\n<li><strong>Almost a quarter</strong> of all vendors were <strong>no longer</strong> in the city, or in the business.</li>\n<li><strong>Out of</strong> the nine hundred vending zones in Chennai, Royapuram/Georgetown Zone <strong>together with</strong> Kodambakkam, in Central Chennai, had lost <strong>largest</strong> number of street vendors, almost five thousand, or nearly <strong>one quarter of all</strong> vendors contacted.</li>\n<li>The report also said that sixty eight vendors died within the period of lockdown. It is, however, unclear if they died of Covid, or unemployment, or anything else.</li>\n<li><strong>Under</strong> six thousand messages, a <strong>fewer</strong> than half, remained undelivered.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>“All those highlighted words, phrases and expressions are nothing, if not connecting numbers” to one another and to their parameters. Then the numbers are not “a meaningless collection of digits” but an overall picture of the vendors’ situation <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>nearly, almost</td>\n<td>a little less than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a little over</td>\n<td>a little more than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a little under, under, fewer than</td>\n<td>less than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>out of</td>\n<td>from a total of</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>together with</td>\n<td>adding one to another</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no longer</td>\n<td>not any more</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Checking the model’s words against the table (my check, with the lecture’s data)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model answer</th>\n<th>Table</th>\n<th>Fit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“a little under twelve thousand” responded</td>\n<td>11,648</td>\n<td>fits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“almost half” of all tracked</td>\n<td>11,648 ÷ 23,154 = 50.3%</td>\n<td>just over half: “about half”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Under six thousand” undelivered</td>\n<td>5,629</td>\n<td>fits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“fewer than half” of the messages</td>\n<td>5,629 ÷ 15,265 = 36.9%</td>\n<td>fits; more exactly “over a third”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Nearly twenty five thousand” contacted</td>\n<td>23,154 tracked</td>\n<td>loose: “over twenty-three thousand”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“a little over five thousand” not reached</td>\n<td>4,796 unreachable</td>\n<td>4,796 is a little <strong>under</strong> five thousand (5,629 is the number of undelivered messages)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“almost five thousand” left the two zones</td>\n<td>3,139 + 1,300 = 4,439</td>\n<td>“about four and a half thousand”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“nearly one quarter of all” contacted</td>\n<td>4,439 ÷ 23,154 = 19.2%</td>\n<td>about one in five</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Almost a quarter” no longer in the city or the business</td>\n<td>left the business: 7,965 ÷ 23,154 = 34.4%; left the city: 2,509 ÷ 23,154 = 10.8%</td>\n<td>about a third left the business; about one in ten left the city</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the model uses the right <strong>kind</strong> of words. Before you speak, check each one against its number.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"walking-an-extra-mile-a-wedding-in-nigeria\">Walking an extra mile: a wedding in Nigeria</h3>\n<p>The second activity (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1235s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:35</a>): “Watch the following video showing a traditional wedding in Nigeria, and write a two minute report on what makes a wedding here different from the ones you know” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=th7yTXQpqy0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the video</a> by Refinery29, 0:00–4:00). The lecture treats Africa as a <strong>cultural</strong> area, not only a geographical one <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>. Every wedding has a decorated venue, guests in particular costumes, rituals of language and body, music, dance, song and a feast, but each culture has them in its own way: vegetarian or non-vegetarian feasts; a wedding guest’s dress in Japan, in India or in China <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>.</p>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:20</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>You can focus on costumes, decorations, ambience, rituals, feast, music and entertainment, guests and venue.</li>\n<li>Your report may have words indicating sequence, words like &quot;first, next, then, before, after, following, preceding, etc.</li>\n<li>It may also have words for rituals, such as bowing, holding or joining hands, sitting, rising, bowing, bending, squatting, prostrating, singing, chanting, offering, receiving, etc. Check their meanings and pronunciation, and use them in appropriate places. Make sure you speak like a radio reporter but see like a television camera, all that is in your view.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind of word</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n<th>Why you need them</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequence (time)</td>\n<td>first, next, then, before, after, following, preceding</td>\n<td>rituals happen in an order <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Position (space)</td>\n<td>next to, beside, before, after</td>\n<td>where guests, bride and groom sit or stand <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rituals and postures</td>\n<td>bowing, holding or joining hands, sitting, rising, bending, squatting, prostrating, singing, chanting, offering, receiving</td>\n<td>actions between two people, or between a person and a deity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Some meanings: <strong>ambience</strong> is the feel of a place; to <strong>squat</strong> is to sit on your heels; to <strong>prostrate</strong> is to lie face down, as a sign of deep respect; to <strong>chant</strong> is to sing or say words again and again in a steady rhythm.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Speak like radio, see like a camera</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A camera notices everything in view: costumes, decorations, who sits where, what happens first and next. Your words must give the listener all of it. “Without them, you cannot present a coherent picture.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"at-an-interview\">At an interview</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An interview is one of the usual places where we must speak in English, as a candidate or as an interviewer <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. You can practise both sides: interview your classmates, friends, colleagues, juniors and seniors, formally as well. “Life is … a huge, big laboratory full of chances.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interview</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=125s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:05</a>: “The word interview means ‘a meeting of people face to face, especially for consultation’.” <strong>Consultation</strong> means checking with each other: is this right, can this be done, will this be acceptable? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a> It is a meeting of people who generally do not work together, on a given subject; today it may also be online <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The same slide: “Besides our academic achievements, our success also depends upon how well we do at interviews. But many people feel frightened, intimidated and worried at interviews.” Certificates bring you jobs, but better jobs, promotions and a faster rise come from good interviews too <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>. Frightened candidates sweat, and cannot recall even their own names or the year they passed BA, BSc or BTech <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">An interview is a conversation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“All interviews are conversations of a formal nature between two or more people who do not know one another very well.” So there is no need to be worried. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-interviews-happen\">Why interviews happen</h3>\n<p>The slide asks <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=325s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:25</a>: “Why does an employer want to conduct an interview? Why does a professor conduct an interview while taking a research scholar? Why do some people interview others before taking them as partners in projects?” In India and many Asian countries there are even interviews before a marriage, to see whether the families and the couple find each other acceptable <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>.</p>\n<p>The slide’s answer <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=435s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:15</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>The interviewer wants to share with the prospective employee, scholar, partner, etc.</li>\n<li>Their expectations.</li>\n<li>They want to see the fitness of the candidates</li>\n<li>Candidates appear at interviews because they want those jobs, etc.</li>\n<li>With their education and experience, they think they are qualified for this job.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Beyond the certificates, the interviewers want to see whether you speak well, have good social manners and can take surprise questions; they may also test your knowledge and experience in key areas <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>. A newspaper interview finds out what a President, a Prime Minister or a celebrity thinks on a subject <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>. Both sides look for what the papers do not show: the candidate asks what is special about this company; the company asks what is special about this person that is not in the <strong>biodata</strong> (the written record of education and experience) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>. So come to it “not with a sense of fright or greed or irritation or intimidation” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"two-film-interviews\">Two film interviews</h3>\n<p>The clips (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=700s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:40</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em><strong>The Pursuit of Happyness</strong></em>, an English film: Chris’s job interview (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5mNxBB6uKXc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the clip</a>, 1:48–4:46).</li>\n<li><em><strong>Pratidwandi</strong></em>, a Bengali film by Satyajit Ray in which the interview takes place in English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yyBVBXTFWVs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the clip</a>, all of it).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>While you watch, note (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=595s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:55</a>): (a) what they were asked; (b) what they said; (c) what you liked; (d) how they said it, with regard to grammar, pronunciation, politeness, etc.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Good and not so good interviewers</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=735s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:15</a>: “Good interviewers draw the best out of you; not so good interviewers ask you for the sake of asking.” Whatever the interviewers are like, “the candidate has got to be good” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"five-qualities-of-a-successful-candidate\">Five qualities of a successful candidate</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=765s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:45</a>: “You may have observed that both of these candidates were: a) Confident, b) Polite, c) Good listeners, d) Honest/Frank, without being rude, e) Appearance. Actually, all successful interviews are the results of these factors.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quality</th>\n<th>In one line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Confident</td>\n<td>you know your subject, especially what you are expected to know</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polite</td>\n<td>you respect the interviewers, and it shows in your language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good listener</td>\n<td>total attention, no distraction, no guessing, mental notes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honest and frank, without being rude</td>\n<td>you tell the truth, politely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appearance</td>\n<td>you dress as this interview expects: clean, comfortable, neither over- nor underdressed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">a) Confident</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=790s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:10</a>: “You must know your subject well. But you should know what you are expected to know. (Supporting certificates, degrees, testimonials, etc.)” A <strong>testimonial</strong> is a letter from someone who vouches for your work. Nobody can give you “2 kilograms of confidence”; it can only be <strong>created</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>. The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=865s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:25</a>: “Confidence comes only through knowledge and experience. Confidence is another name for your faith in yourself, or in another person, or in any institution.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Chris says “I don’t know” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Chris raises his hand and asks the committee to let him say something.</li>\n<li>He says, in the lecturer’s words: it is possible that he does not know the answers to all possible questions; then he will tell them he does not know, and he will do his best to find the answer.</li>\n<li>He says it confidently and politely <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> confidence is not knowing everything; it is faith that you can find out, said politely.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">b) Polite</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=945s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:45</a>: “Respect for the other person, respect for interviewers. This mutual respect shows best in language. You must listen carefully, must speak slowly, do not interrupt, do not be distracted, do not feel irritated if at all interrupted etc.” Respect shows in your tone, your choice of words, your sentences, and how you address people <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>. If you must interrupt, raise your hand and take permission first, as Chris does <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The next slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1115s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:35</a>: “Then there are words indicating respect, words like thanks, please, sorry, pardon me, etc. Some interviewers sometimes can be very rude and provocative. But if you stay unprovoked then even not answering can go in your favour.” In the talk the lecturer adds <em>Can I say something?</em> and <em>May I please interrupt you?</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Redundant and provocative questions (the lecture’s examples from <em>Pratidwandi</em>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>redundant</strong> question asks what the interviewers already know. “What is your age?” “When did you pass BSc?” All of it is in the biodata. Never say “that is a stupid question”; answer politely and respectfully <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>provocative</strong> question is meant to make you angry. One interviewer asks, “Are you a communist?”, a personal question that has nothing to do with the job. The candidate answers respectfully: “One does not have to be a communist to admire the people of Vietnam.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> do not allow yourself to be provoked or irritated by unrelated questions, provocative questions or interruptions <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">c) Good listener</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1135s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:55</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Total attention</li>\n<li>No distraction</li>\n<li>No guessing</li>\n<li>Taking mental note of key points (in an online interview, you can take proper notes without losing the thread of conversation)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>What each means <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Total attention</strong>: listen as you listen to your dear ones at important moments, so that one listening is enough.</li>\n<li><strong>No distraction</strong>: no checking messages, no crossword puzzles; good eye contact, easy and relaxed <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>No guessing</strong>: let the interviewer complete the sentence, wait “a decent one or two seconds”, and then answer <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mental notes</strong>: just as you remember which gate your flight leaves from, your mind notes what matters. Online, you can make real notes <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">When you do not understand</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1265s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:05</a>: “If however, there is a point, or a word, or an expression that for some reason you do not understand, seek clarification.” Apologise, raise your hand, take permission, then ask: “Please pardon me, I do not understand the question. Can you tell me again?” Understand the question before you answer it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">d) Honest/frank, without being rude</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1405s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:25</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:40</a>: “Many people feel that at any interview you come across best when you are honest and truthful.” “Many more people believe that you need not always be honest, you are unlikely to be caught telling lies or being dishonest. By being honest you might lose the present chance, but you may be better prepared for greater success with all other chances.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Both film candidates were frank: one said he had been at the police station; the other said he had studied medicine but quit, and he said why <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>. The lecturer follows the wise (Gandhi, Buddha, Christ, Mohammed, Nanak, Valluvar): truth is the best preparation. But the choice is yours <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>. It helps to be honest and frank without being rude, and to stay polite <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">e) Appearance</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:40</a>: “You should know what kind of dress would be considered appropriate for the interview you are going to appear at.” There are no hard and fast rules: what is appropriate varies with culture, gender, profession and community <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>. Lawyers in India wear a black coat even in summer; doctors and scientists are mostly in white; air traffic staff, the police and the army each have their own colours <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>. “Do not be overdressed. Do not be underdressed either.” What you wear should be <strong>clean</strong> and <strong>comfortable</strong>, and should distract neither the interviewers nor you <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Are the film interviews unreal? In the lecturer’s experience of dozens of interviews on both sides of the table, real interviews happen just like that. Some interviewers ask relevant questions; others ask random ones, even when the answers are already on paper. A good candidate takes care either way <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>.</p>\n<p>The activity (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1565s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:05</a>): “The following questions were asked by a selection committee recruiting computer maintenance engineers.” The questions themselves are not shown in the video. Speak or write your answers, and hold mock interviews with friends, colleagues or family. “Success in any walk of life is guaranteed, provided we pursue it with determination and honesty.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"group-discussion-1-behaviour-and-language\">Group discussion 1: behaviour and language</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many companies and institutions call candidates for a job, a scholarship or a position and ask them to talk about a topic among themselves. An <strong>observer</strong> from the employers watches your performance, your behaviour, how you conduct yourself and how you get along with others, and decides who would be the best prospective employee <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. GDs are also a method of work, of solving problems and of reaching consensus, in industry, universities, schools and colleges <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Group discussion (GD)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>group discussion</strong> (<strong>GD</strong>) is a discussion of a topic by a group of people, often watched by an observer, used to reach consensus or to judge the participants <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. <strong>Consensus</strong> is an agreement that the whole group accepts. The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>: “Group Discussion is a very popular tool to reach consensus.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Consensus is easy where nobody disagrees. The real work is where opinions differ. The lecture’s example: “Should we continue learning and teaching online?” There are points for and against; in the end someone must decide, and the decision comes out best “when it is the group’s decision” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-goes-wrong-in-groups\">What goes wrong in groups</h3>\n<p>The same slide: “Problems of good behaviour: impatience, cross-talking, arrogance, jealousy, inattention, etc.” (<strong>Cross-talking</strong> is talking while someone else is talking.)</p>\n<p>“Character … shows when you are all by yourself”, but “your personality reveals itself when you are in a group” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. In a group none of us stays the same. We become cooperative, helpful, congenial, amiable, or the other way round: irritable, quarrelsome, hungry for attention, jealous, envious, arrogant, easily distracted <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>.</p>\n<p>The cartoon (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>): one man stands and talks on and on. The others think: “His points are invalid”, “When will I get to speak”, “I should change my view he could be right”, “I am not interested”. Every group has people who speak longer than they ought to, people who speak less, and people who speak just for their turn. We must learn to manage this diversity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</p>\n<p>The video clip (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>; <em>Learn English with Rebecca</em>, engVid, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YY2yjEEoB3U\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the clip</a>, 1:13–3:36): four people, watched by a teacher, discuss whether learning in a classroom or learning online is better <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good: they allow each other to speak, and they make new points.</li>\n<li>Not so good: one man speaks so fast that he is hard to follow without subtitles; one woman is impatient and has to be reminded to let the other speaker finish <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"advantages-of-a-gd\">Advantages of a GD</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:50</a>: “Makes a team. Promotes good group behaviours like cooperation, exchange of ideas, formation of functional groups, learning and optimum use of resources.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Makes a team</strong>: “we are all marvellous people individually, but do we make a great team?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Good group behaviour</strong> begins with <strong>mutual respect</strong>: “these are my colleagues I cannot do well unless they do well” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>. It draws out cooperation and the exchange of ideas <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Functional groups</strong>: specialists in computer applications, computer science, electronics, logistics and communications see one another’s points of view, and so see what the group needs <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Learning</strong>: from colleagues, friends and family, who know things you do not <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimum use of resources</strong>: one person has only so much money, energy or ideas; together we get the best returns from what we have <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four walls</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s picture <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>: one wall, however strong, does not make a house. Four walls that meet at the corners hold up a roof, and then the walls and the roof become unbreakable. A team becomes a team when every member keeps the team’s goal, the team spirit, above themselves.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-language-of-arrogance\">The language of arrogance</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>, “Language-related issues”:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Language of arrogance: I; My opinion; Talking out of turn; Talking long; Insensitivity, like telling others, ‘you should’; Presenting oneself as a superior- I could’ve, I see it, keep quiet, shut up, etc.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Humble people never tell you they are humble. You know the arrogant by their language: it is <strong>I-centred</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>. The lecture’s examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“When I was your age, I used to work 26 hours a day.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></li>\n<li>“In my opinion we must do it now, we must not wait.”</li>\n<li>Talking out of turn; taking a long time; always preaching: “you should do this, you should not speak that way” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>; always finding fault, although none of us is perfect and each of us has strengths to bring <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>.</li>\n<li>Boasting: “I could have taken 100 kilograms in a hanky and could have thrown it two kilometres away.” Rude orders: “keep quiet”, “shut up”, “allow me to finish”, “I will speak first” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"language-don-ts\">Language don’ts</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1115s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:35</a>, “Language - Don’ts”:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>His/her points are invalid. · Your argument doesn’t make any sense. · Avoid words like gonna, wanna, ya etc. · Do not use pronouns in place of nouns (e.g: she, he in place of their names) · When will I get to speak · I am right, I know everything · I am not interested</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you really think an argument makes no sense, there are polite ways to say so: “Can you say it differently?” “Can you come again?” “Sorry, we do not understand you.” “Can we say it this way?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a> Slang (<em>gonna, wanna</em>) is fine within your group of friends, but not in a formal situation <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Name first, pronoun after</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Never use pronoun before you have used noun.” “I told her”: who is <em>her</em>? “She is bringing it”: who is <em>she</em>? Say “Aishwarya is bringing it”, “Ramakant is bringing it”. After that, <em>he</em> and <em>she</em> are clear: “Oh, he has already started; he called me before leaving for office.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Body language (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1195s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:55</a>, with the cartoon “Playing the Blame Game”): “Avoid gestures like pointing fingers, tapping the desk with the pen, table thumping, looking here and there, etc.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"good-group-good-language\">Good group, good language</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>, “Language of team spirit”: <em>Shall we, Can we, Should we, May I, You first,</em> small sentences, keeping time, remembering names and points.</p>\n<p>The lecture’s examples: “Shall we try this idea?” “Shall we find out?” “Can we wait?” “Should we try in this direction?” “Please permit me.” “May I suggest something?” “No, no, you first please, I can wait.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a> Speak in small sentences, slowly enough for everyone, within your allotted time. Remember names and points, so that you need not keep asking “Pardon me, what did you say?”: that “leaves a very bad taste”, takes time, and shows you were not attentive <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"likely-places-of-error\">Likely places of error</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:10</a>: “Many learners do not use: polite expressions like can, may; question sentences like shall we, should we, etc.; use of more pronouns or group identity: we, our company, our team.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instead of</th>\n<th>Say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Do this. Do that.</td>\n<td>Shall we do this? Should we do this? Is it all right to look at it this way? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I will do it.</td>\n<td>I can do it. Can I have a chance? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is for me (or for the company) to decide.</td>\n<td>It is for us to decide where we want to be five years from now. Can our company be there in 3 years? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“I will do it” is all right, “but it is not very polite”. <em>Can</em> and <em>may</em> as polite words were studied in <a href=\"#/english2/w05/can-and-could\">Week 5</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"respect-and-disrespect\">Respect and disrespect</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1345s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:25</a>: “Language of respect: All sections can be invited. Language of disrespect: English teachers, poor people, women. No use or limited use of polite words like can, shall, could, would, seem, look, feel.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Disrespectful</th>\n<th>Respectful</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Even women can come.</td>\n<td>Everyone can try.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Even people from South India can try speaking Hindi.</td>\n<td>All of us can try.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Well, even English teachers can come to this meeting.</td>\n<td>All who are interested are welcome.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Let us do something for poor people.</td>\n<td>Let us work for everyone, rich or poor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Women should get due respect.</td>\n<td>Let us give a chance to everyone, men or women.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The word <em>even</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Even women can come” sounds as if you are “making a great concession” (granting a favour). The respectful way includes everyone: all regions, all genders, all castes, classes, communities and cultures <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Without polite words (<em>shall, could, would, may, might, feel, seem</em>), English can sound rude. They come to you not by learning them by heart but from “a proper attitude”: the attitude of respect, the attitude of finding out <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phrases-for-a-discussion\">Phrases for a discussion</h3>\n<p>The slides “Language of respect” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1455s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:15</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n<th>Phrases</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Asking for opinions</td>\n<td>Could you tell me …; Do you think/feel …; May I ask you …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Giving your opinion</td>\n<td>In my opinion/view …; Sorry to interrupt you, but …; As far as I can see / I am concerned …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreeing with an opinion</td>\n<td>I (quite) agree; That’s exactly how I see it; Yes, indeed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Giving an explanation</td>\n<td>What I mean is …; Well, the reason is …; Just let me explain …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polite disagreement</td>\n<td>I am sorry, I can’t agree; I don’t think so; You don’t really mean that, do you?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">“My opinion” on both lists? (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>My opinion</em> is on the slide of arrogance, and <em>In my opinion</em> on the slide of respect. The difference is in the use. “In my opinion we must do it now, we must not wait” pushes your view over everyone else’s. “In my opinion …”, followed by your reason and offered after others have spoken, is a polite way to give a view.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"etiquette\">Etiquette</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1475s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:35</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Etiquette: Best shows in language. · Soft words rather than shouting or whispering. · Simple sentences- Mostly mono or bi-syllabic words in an inclusive tone: We can all learn · In addition to prevailing norms of dress</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Etiquette</strong> is the accepted rules of polite behaviour. <em>Mono-syllabic</em> words have one syllable and <em>bi-syllabic</em> words two. Know how to stand, sit and talk; be easily audible and visible to all, neither shouting nor whispering; and dress properly for the occasion <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>.</p>\n<p>The activity (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:05</a>): observe a group discussion at your workplace or institution, and note (a) good practices, (b) not such good practices, (c) examples of language, some that you may avoid and some that you may use. Then share your work with friends and reviewers. Recording the discussion helps <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"group-discussion-2-introducing-and-inviting\">Group discussion 2: introducing and inviting</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many of us have to <strong>invite</strong> speakers, <strong>introduce</strong> speakers, or, as speakers, <strong>invite questions</strong> during or after our own talks <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. In the same few minutes, some speakers “strike a chord” of sympathy and understanding with their audience, while others, however learned, leave it bored, uninterested or even hostile <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. The difference has little to do with knowledge (although without knowledge you can say nothing). It is <strong>attitude</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The right attitude</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Are you humble, or proud and arrogant? Do you treat yourself “with a healthy amount of contempt”, or have you “fallen in love with yourself”? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a> You may be self-confident and proud of your achievements, with some humility and politeness <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>. A good view of oneself “is a critical view”: aware of your achievements and your failures, your pluses and your minuses. Then you get along well in any crowd <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"introducing-your-own-group\">Introducing your own group</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two friends, two gifts (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture imagines a group of five friends and describes two of them.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>One friend never writes English correctly, but he is a great group-maker: he can take many people along.</li>\n<li>Another writes very good English (give him ten minutes and he will produce a party manifesto or a brochure single-handed), but he always has to be reminded that it is time for the bus.</li>\n<li>Introducing them to a VIP: “This is X; he still makes mistakes in grammar, but he is wonderful in bonding people. And I have here this other friend, who corrects everyone’s grammar, but who always has to be reminded that it is time for the bus.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> with a little humour, a little wit and a little humility, you bring out the pluses and the minuses of everyone in your group <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>, “Presenting on behalf of group”:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Introducing your group · Language of introduction · Complimentary but not exaggerated · Focusing on individual achievements and group membership- One is a good painter but not good at remembering names, etc. · The job done by the group. · Materials, methods used by the group.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The language of introduction <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple forms: “This is X and Y.” “On my left …, on my right …”</li>\n<li>Be <strong>self-effacing</strong> (keep yourself in the background): “We are six of us. I am introducing my friends because they thought they were very busy; only I have some free time.” Do not say “I am the captain of the team”; it is understood <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>.</li>\n<li>Find something good about everybody: “This person may not know everything, but there will hardly be a question which he will not do his utmost to answer.”</li>\n<li>Be <strong>complimentary but not hyperbolic</strong> (not exaggerated). Do not say “he knows every word in the dictionary”, even if he does; say how he learned them, what he did that others have not <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A music group (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“We are all amateurs. Every Saturday, we meet in the temple. Here is my friend on the left, Muruganandam; he is wonderful with the ghatam. And here is another person; he can make even gods cry with his veena.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>If the veena player really is that good, say so.</li>\n<li>If he is not, do not criticise him: “he is still making brave attempts to make gods cry when he plays veena” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>.</li>\n<li>In a sentence or two you can show the group’s priorities, what it is weak in and what it is strong in, if you have done your homework <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> humour keeps the introduction kind and honest at the same time. (The ghatam is a clay-pot drum and the veena a stringed instrument, both of Carnatic music.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"introducing-a-guest\">Introducing a guest</h3>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not read out the CV</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Reading out the guest’s bio-note or <strong>curriculum vitae</strong> (CV: the record of education, jobs and prizes) is the most boring introduction there can be <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>. The audience already knows the public facts; that is why they came <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Instead, find what is <strong>unique</strong> to this speaker, what is not in the public domain <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A film star: how many sleepless nights he spent learning his dialogues, and how he still acted naturally as an old man or a beggar <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>.</li>\n<li>A governor (the lecturer’s example): besides “Governor, Chief Secretary, MP, MLA”, tell how once, travelling from Delhi to Bombay, he gave his berth to a group of young women who were travelling in an emergency without seats, spread his newspapers on the floor, and slept there until Bombay <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. “A great person is great only when he creates opportunities for others.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gandhi: an exaggeration and two fresh facts (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Exaggerated: “Mr Gandhi is greater than God.” This only makes fun of him.</li>\n<li>Better: “Gandhi was also an engineer; look at the way he designed the charkha (the spinning wheel). It took him two and a half years.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></li>\n<li>Or: at Tolstoy Farm in South Africa there was no shower, so he hung a bucket from the ceiling with a rope; when he or his sons pulled the rope, the bucket sprayed water on them <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> true facts that are known, but “not so well known”, make a memorable introduction. They come from finding out about the person <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The clip (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=965s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:05</a>; <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0tiHZI3O3lM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the video</a>, 0:00–3:15): a young executive introduces Bill Gates in Kuwait. She says all that is relevant, and says it differently: a great man’s work for his company has also created opportunities for many people who are not even remotely connected with it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>. Any of us can do this if we set our minds to it and give it time: find out, make one or two drafts, rehearse once or twice. Then the introduction is “very powerful, brief, effective” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"welcoming-the-audience\">Welcoming the audience</h3>\n<p>The slides “Welcome your audience &amp; introduction” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:50</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1125s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:45</a>. The labels in brackets are the slide’s; the first two are not labelled:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Welcome to [name of company or event]. My name is [name] and I am the [job title or background information].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Thank you for coming today. I’m [name] and I look forward to talking with you today about [your topic].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(formal)</td>\n<td>Good morning/afternoon ladies and gentlemen. I’d like to quickly introduce myself. I am [name] from [company or position].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(formal)</td>\n<td>On behalf of [name of company], I’d like to welcome you today. For those of you who don’t already know me, my name is [name] and I am [job title or background].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(informal)</td>\n<td>Hi everyone. I’m [name and background]. I’m glad to be here with you today. Now let’s get started.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Opening a discussion</td>\n<td>So you know we are talking about discussion and the topic is …. Who wants to go first?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>You may depart from these and strike new patterns, but not so new that the audience is baffled: a creative opening “just changes a parameter or two” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"inviting-and-answering-questions\">Inviting and answering questions</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1135s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:55</a> lists the common situations in the work-life of any manager or scientist: inviting speakers; introducing speakers; inviting questions; answering questions at a formal meeting; group language <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Answering a provocative question</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Do not say “that is a stupid question”. Take it in your stride and, without being rude, tell the person that the question could not be answered, and why <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>When you invite questions, the same things help as in an introduction: a little humour, a little wit, and above all remembering and finding out <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-great-introductions-share\">What great introductions share</h3>\n<p>Watch the first few minutes of great speeches critically whenever you have time <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>. You will find three common features <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Finding out</strong>: the speakers have done some research, and say something not already well known, so they are heard with interest and attention.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple, easy language</strong>: small sentences and everyday words, so that the words themselves are not a challenge <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Slow, easy delivery</strong>, when you are not excited or tense.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These help in introductions, conclusions and inviting questions, whether you are the speaker, the <strong>compère</strong> (the host who introduces the speakers and links the parts of an event) or the person presiding <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>.</p>\n<p>The activity (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:50</a>): watch George Bernard Shaw’s speech in honour of Albert Einstein (0:00–1:52; the video linked on the slide is now private), and make one list of things you like and another of things you do not like so much in such introductions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Radio report</strong>: only words carry the picture: what happened, what was said, where, how the place and people looked. Say all you can in the time; be fair to every part of the topic. Practise: watch (without, then with subtitles), note names, special words, the sequence and differences, report in 3 → 2½ → 2 minutes, record, take feedback, redo.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear numbers</strong>: 13/30, 14/40, 15/50, 17/70, 19/90 get confused; say all three sounds of <em>five</em>; letters (P, G, V, C, D, E) too. <strong>THIR</strong>-ty but thir-<strong>TEEN</strong>; in abbreviations the last letter is stressed (U-<strong>K</strong>, U-S-<strong>A</strong>); per-<strong>CENT</strong>. Read 7,965 as “seven thousand nine hundred <strong>and</strong> sixty-five”. Deliver numbers slowly and carefully.</li>\n<li><strong>Words around numbers</strong>: <em>much, many, little, few</em>; <em>all, none, mostly, rarely, hardly ever</em>; <em>dozens, lakhs, millions, quintals, scores</em>; <em>first, last, penultimate, greater than</em>; <em>per cent, portion, proportion, ratio, partly, wholly</em>. Digits are read differently in different cultures (“one double zero”).</li>\n<li><strong>Relative values</strong>: <em>whereas, on one hand … on the other, out of, as high as, as low as</em>; <em>nearly, almost, a little over, a little under, almost half, over a quarter</em>. Compare with a quarter, a third or a half, then choose the word. Never claim more than the data say.</li>\n<li><strong>Time</strong>: plan for 110 seconds when you have 120; do not overpack.</li>\n<li><strong>Interview</strong>: “a meeting of people face to face, especially for consultation”; a formal conversation. Five qualities: confident (knowledge and experience), polite (respect in language; stay unprovoked), good listener (total attention, no distraction, no guessing, mental notes; seek clarification), honest and frank without being rude, appropriate appearance (clean, comfortable, neither over- nor underdressed).</li>\n<li><strong>GD</strong>: a tool to reach consensus and to select people. Advantages: a team, cooperation, exchange of ideas, functional groups, learning, optimum use of resources. Avoid <em>I, my opinion, you should, keep quiet, shut up</em>, “your argument doesn’t make any sense”, slang, a pronoun before the name, pointing fingers. Use <em>shall we, can we, should we, may I, you first</em>; <em>we, our team</em>; <em>can</em> rather than <em>will</em>; respect all (“everyone can try”, not “even women can come”).</li>\n<li><strong>Introductions and questions</strong>: a humble, self-critical attitude; for your group, self-effacing and complimentary but not hyperbolic; for a guest, the unique fact, not the CV; set phrases to welcome an audience; answer provocative questions calmly; research, simple language, slow delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Mark the stressed syllable in <em>thirteen, thirty, sixteen</em> and <em>USA</em>, and say why it matters for a speaker.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>thirteen</em>: thir-<strong>TEEN</strong>, the second syllable, with a long <em>ee</em> (teeth show, lips spread).</li>\n<li><em>thirty</em>: <strong>THIR</strong>-ty, the first syllable.</li>\n<li><em>sixteen</em>: six-<strong>TEEN</strong>, the second syllable (like every <em>-teen</em> number).</li>\n<li><em>USA</em>: U-S-<strong>A</strong>, the last letter.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the two syllables of <em>thirteen</em> and <em>thirty</em> differ only in the last one, so the stress is the main thing that keeps 13 and 30 apart. A misheard number can cause “serious irreversible” losses, like the air crash in Lecture 7.2.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Read aloud 23,154 and 15,265, and explain the word <em>and</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>23,154 = twenty-three thousand / one hundred / <strong>and</strong> fifty-four.</li>\n<li>15,265 = fifteen thousand / two hundred / <strong>and</strong> sixty-five.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “twenty-three thousand one hundred and fifty-four”; “fifteen thousand two hundred and sixty-five”. The <em>and</em> before the last part tells the listener that the number is about to end (Lecture 7.3).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A data slide says: 27% of girls marry early, 40% are anaemic, and 46% of primary schools lack basic facilities. Turn each figure into words a radio listener can follow.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>27% against a quarter (25%): 27 is more than 25, so “<strong>over a quarter</strong>”.</li>\n<li>40% against a third (100 ÷ 3 = 33.3%): 40 is more than 33.3, so “<strong>over one third</strong>” (exactly, “two in five”).</li>\n<li>46% against a half (50%): 46 is a little less than 50, so “<strong>nearly half</strong>”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> over a quarter; over one third; nearly half, the very words of the model report in Lecture 7.2.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Of 15,265 messages sent to street vendors, 5,629 were not delivered. Which is the better sentence for a report, and why? (A) “5,629 vendors had no phone connection.” (B) “Out of over fifteen thousand messages sent, more than a third, over five and a half thousand, were never delivered.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(A) claims a cause. The data do not say why the messages failed: the phones may have been switched off, low on power or poorly connected (Lecture 7.3).</li>\n<li>(B) states only what the data say, and gives the relative value with <em>out of</em> and <em>more than a third</em>.</li>\n<li>Check the fraction: 5,629 ÷ 15,265 = 0.369 = 36.9%, which is more than a third (33.3%) and less than a half.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (B). It claims nothing beyond the data, and it shows how the numbers relate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">At an interview you hear a question you do not understand, and later one you cannot answer. What should you say each time?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Not understood: seek clarification politely. Raise your hand, take permission, and say: “Please pardon me, I do not understand the question. Can you tell me again?” Do not guess.</li>\n<li>Cannot answer: be honest and frank, without being rude, like Chris in <em>The Pursuit of Happyness</em>: say that you do not know, and that you will do your best to find the answer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> clarification for the first; an honest “I do not know, but I will find out” for the second. Both show politeness and confidence.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make these fit for a group discussion: (a) “Your argument doesn’t make any sense.” (b) “I will do it.” (c) “Even women can come.” (d) “She is bringing the laptop.” (said before anyone knows who “she” is)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) Do not attack the speaker; ask: “Sorry, we do not understand you. Can you say it differently?” or “Can we say it this way?”</li>\n<li>(b) <em>Will</em> is all right but not very polite; use <em>can</em>: “I can do it. Can I have a chance?”</li>\n<li>(c) <em>Even</em> sounds like a favour; include everyone: “Everyone can try” or “All who are interested are welcome.”</li>\n<li>(d) Name first, pronoun after: “Aishwarya is bringing the laptop.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> polite questions, <em>can</em> rather than <em>will</em>, inclusive words, and the name before the pronoun (Lecture 7.5).</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
